1
00:00:00,321 --> 00:00:03,301
Let me start where a working data scientist starts,

2
00:00:03,422 --> 00:00:05,400
with a decision somebody has to make.

3
00:00:05,601 --> 00:00:08,722
A bank receives a loan application and has to decide

4
00:00:08,584 --> 00:00:09,596
whether to lend.

5
00:00:09,789 --> 00:00:12,604
Behind that decision sits a table of past loans,

6
00:00:12,655 --> 00:00:15,671
and the one we will use is real: 1,000 loans from

7
00:00:15,532 --> 00:00:18,453
a German bank, published for research, with twenty

8
00:00:18,315 --> 00:00:20,800
facts about each borrower and a final column that

9
00:00:20,662 --> 00:00:23,206
records whether the loan was repaid or went bad.

10
00:00:23,419 --> 00:00:26,128
Seven hundred were repaid, and three hundred were

11
00:00:25,990 --> 00:00:26,588
not.

12
00:00:26,792 --> 00:00:29,725
Before any formula, notice what this table must

13
00:00:29,587 --> 00:00:32,071
contain, because it is what you will need for your

14
00:00:31,934 --> 00:00:32,731
own problem.

15
00:00:32,936 --> 00:00:35,856
Each row is one example, each column is a feature

16
00:00:35,719 --> 00:00:38,310
you knew at decision time, and one column is the

17
00:00:38,172 --> 00:00:39,701
target you want to predict.

18
00:00:39,929 --> 00:00:42,437
We will keep eight features, such as the balance

19
00:00:42,299 --> 00:00:44,914
of the current account, the loan duration, the

20
00:00:44,775 --> 00:00:47,166
amount, the savings and the age.

21
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:50,420
And we will hide 200 rows from the model, so that

22
00:00:50,283 --> 00:00:52,661
at the end we can test it on loans it has never

23
00:00:52,523 --> 00:00:53,109
seen.

24
00:00:54,559 --> 00:00:56,006
Which model, then?

25
00:00:56,231 --> 00:00:58,999
The first question is what kind of answer you need.

26
00:00:59,226 --> 00:01:02,017
If the target is a number, such as how many bikes

27
00:01:01,879 --> 00:01:04,565
will be rented at five o'clock, that is a regression

28
00:01:04,426 --> 00:01:06,853
problem, and linear regression is the place to

29
00:01:06,714 --> 00:01:07,406
start.

30
00:01:07,634 --> 00:01:10,319
If the target is a yes or no, such as will this

31
00:01:10,181 --> 00:01:13,433
loan go bad, that is classification, and logistic

32
00:01:13,294 --> 00:01:15,119
regression is the place to start.

33
00:01:15,346 --> 00:01:18,350
If there are several classes, you reach for softmax,

34
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:19,848
which we covered last time.

35
00:01:20,042 --> 00:01:22,893
The second question is what you need from the answer.

36
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:25,639
When a regulator or a doctor has to understand

37
00:01:25,502 --> 00:01:28,282
the decision, a linear or logistic model gives

38
00:01:28,143 --> 00:01:30,852
you a readable coefficient for every feature, and

39
00:01:30,714 --> 00:01:33,139
that readability is often the reason it wins.

40
00:01:33,367 --> 00:01:35,569
When the relationship is clearly not a straight

41
00:01:35,431 --> 00:01:38,069
line and you have the data to prove it, a tree

42
00:01:37,931 --> 00:01:40,463
ensemble or a neural network will fit better.

43
00:01:40,691 --> 00:01:43,163
But fit the regression first, because it is the

44
00:01:43,025 --> 00:01:45,852
baseline every other model has to beat, and because

45
00:01:45,714 --> 00:01:48,446
the last layer of that network is a logistic regression

46
00:01:48,309 --> 00:01:48,919
anyway.

47
00:01:50,404 --> 00:01:52,984
So let us build linear regression properly, on

48
00:01:52,845 --> 00:01:55,259
five points small enough to do by hand: x from

49
00:01:55,121 --> 00:01:58,360
one to five, and y equal to two, two point eight,

50
00:01:58,458 --> 00:02:01,285
four point one, four point nine, and six point

51
00:02:01,147 --> 00:02:01,639
two.

52
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:05,481
Any line, w x plus b, misses each point by some

53
00:02:05,342 --> 00:02:07,732
residual, and if we square those residuals and

54
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:10,480
add them up we get a single number, the loss, that

55
00:02:10,342 --> 00:02:12,237
says how badly the line is doing.

56
00:02:12,441 --> 00:02:15,104
Drag the line, and the loss changes with it.

57
00:02:15,292 --> 00:02:17,918
To find the best line we set both derivatives of

58
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,336
the loss to zero, which gives two equations in

59
00:02:20,198 --> 00:02:22,753
two unknowns, and they solve in closed form.

60
00:02:22,979 --> 00:02:25,781
The slope is the covariance of x and y divided

61
00:02:25,644 --> 00:02:28,458
by the variance of x, and the intercept is chosen

62
00:02:28,321 --> 00:02:30,652
so that the line passes through the mean point.

63
00:02:30,875 --> 00:02:34,209
With our numbers, x bar is 3 and y bar is 4.

64
00:02:34,436 --> 00:02:37,770
The covariance sum comes to 10.5 and the variance

65
00:02:37,632 --> 00:02:38,501
sum to 10.

66
00:02:38,696 --> 00:02:44,529
So w is 1.05, and b is 4 minus 1.05 times 3, which

67
00:02:44,391 --> 00:02:45,885
is 0.85.

68
00:02:46,111 --> 00:02:51,049
The loss at that line is 0.075, down from 91.1

69
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:52,629
for a flat line through zero.

70
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:55,378
For more than one input the same idea is written

71
00:02:55,239 --> 00:02:59,221
once, in matrix form: X transpose X, times theta,

72
00:02:59,354 --> 00:03:01,085
equals X transpose y.

73
00:03:01,279 --> 00:03:03,823
These are the normal equations, and solving them

74
00:03:03,684 --> 00:03:05,839
gives the plane that fits a cloud of points in

75
00:03:05,701 --> 00:03:08,280
three dimensions, or the hyperplane that fits a

76
00:03:08,142 --> 00:03:10,756
table with six columns, which is exactly what we

77
00:03:10,618 --> 00:03:11,594
are about to do.

78
00:03:12,333 --> 00:03:15,431
And gradient descent, from lesson one, arrives

79
00:03:15,293 --> 00:03:16,410
at the same answer.

80
00:03:16,637 --> 00:03:19,110
The gradient of the loss is twice the residuals

81
00:03:18,972 --> 00:03:21,646
weighted by x, and twice the residuals on their

82
00:03:21,508 --> 00:03:22,036
own.

83
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:26,245
Four hundred small steps from zero, and w is 1.05,

84
00:03:26,378 --> 00:03:30,809
b is 0.849: the closed form, found by walking downhill.

85
00:03:32,279 --> 00:03:33,526
Now the real job.

86
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:36,472
A bike-sharing company wants to know how many bikes

87
00:03:36,333 --> 00:03:38,794
will be rented in a given hour, so it can move

88
00:03:38,656 --> 00:03:40,186
bikes to where they are needed.

89
00:03:40,413 --> 00:03:45,610
The data is real: 17,379 hours over two years,

90
00:03:45,472 --> 00:03:47,992
and for each hour the temperature, the humidity,

91
00:03:47,854 --> 00:03:50,433
the wind, whether it was a working day, and the

92
00:03:50,295 --> 00:03:53,594
number of rentals, which averaged 189.

93
00:03:53,455 --> 00:03:56,601
The hour of the day goes in as a sine and a cosine,

94
00:03:56,462 --> 00:03:59,160
so that eleven at night sits next to midnight rather

95
00:03:59,021 --> 00:04:00,386
than twenty-three steps away.

96
00:04:00,583 --> 00:04:04,754
We solve the normal equations on 13,903 training

97
00:04:04,616 --> 00:04:06,559
hours and read the coefficients.

98
00:04:06,753 --> 00:04:09,921
Each extra degree of temperature adds about 6.7

99
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:12,763
rentals an hour, and each point of humidity removes

100
00:04:12,625 --> 00:04:13,436
about one.

101
00:04:13,661 --> 00:04:16,170
Those are sentences the operations team can act

102
00:04:16,031 --> 00:04:19,283
on, and no deep model hands them to you so directly.

103
00:04:19,458 --> 00:04:20,458
Did it work?

104
00:04:20,651 --> 00:04:24,788
On the 3,476 held-out hours the typical error is

105
00:04:24,649 --> 00:04:28,644
97 rentals, the root mean square error is 140,

106
00:04:28,788 --> 00:04:31,875
and the model explains 0.42 of the variance.

107
00:04:32,088 --> 00:04:34,973
That is a useful baseline and an honest one, and

108
00:04:34,835 --> 00:04:36,719
we will come back to what it gets wrong.

109
00:04:38,184 --> 00:04:39,914
Now the yes-or-no question.

110
00:04:40,139 --> 00:04:43,095
Here are ten students, the hours each one studied,

111
00:04:43,158 --> 00:04:44,416
and whether they passed.

112
00:04:44,643 --> 00:04:47,341
A straight line predicts numbers below zero and

113
00:04:47,202 --> 00:04:49,734
above one, which no probability can be.

114
00:04:49,950 --> 00:04:52,211
So we take the linear score and pass it through

115
00:04:52,073 --> 00:04:55,123
the sigmoid, one over one plus e to the minus score,

116
00:04:55,268 --> 00:04:57,718
and now the output lives between zero and one.

117
00:04:57,917 --> 00:05:00,425
Read it backwards and the score has a meaning.

118
00:05:00,653 --> 00:05:03,892
The log of p over one minus p, the log-odds, is

119
00:05:03,754 --> 00:05:07,760
exactly w x plus b, so each extra unit of x multiplies

120
00:05:07,622 --> 00:05:09,270
the odds by e to the w.

121
00:05:09,485 --> 00:05:11,958
That is why doctors and banks like this model:

122
00:05:12,115 --> 00:05:14,682
every coefficient is an odds ratio they can read

123
00:05:14,544 --> 00:05:15,201
aloud.

124
00:05:15,417 --> 00:05:16,616
How do we fit it?

125
00:05:16,832 --> 00:05:18,302
Not with squared error.

126
00:05:18,530 --> 00:05:21,156
We maximise the likelihood of the labels we actually

127
00:05:21,018 --> 00:05:24,411
saw, the product of p for every pass and one minus

128
00:05:24,273 --> 00:05:25,578
p for every fail.

129
00:05:25,773 --> 00:05:28,658
Take the log, flip the sign, and the loss is the

130
00:05:28,521 --> 00:05:31,583
cross-entropy from last lesson, for two classes.

131
00:05:31,750 --> 00:05:35,037
Differentiate, and the sigmoid's derivative cancels

132
00:05:34,899 --> 00:05:35,650
the log's.

133
00:05:35,876 --> 00:05:39,752
The gradient is X transpose times p minus y: prediction

134
00:05:39,614 --> 00:05:42,665
minus truth, weighted by the inputs, exactly the

135
00:05:42,527 --> 00:05:43,585
softmax rule.

136
00:05:43,812 --> 00:05:46,580
There is no closed form this time, so gradient

137
00:05:46,442 --> 00:05:47,783
descent does the work.

138
00:05:47,958 --> 00:05:52,778
Fitted, w is 2.6 and b is -7.16.

139
00:05:53,005 --> 00:05:56,741
The fifty-percent point is 2.75 hours; two hours

140
00:05:56,602 --> 00:06:00,443
of study gives a 0.12 chance of passing, and four

141
00:06:00,305 --> 00:06:01,999
hours gives 0.96.

142
00:06:03,434 --> 00:06:04,575
Back to the bank.

143
00:06:04,799 --> 00:06:07,178
We standardise the eight features so that each

144
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:09,713
is measured in standard deviations, then train

145
00:06:09,575 --> 00:06:12,520
logistic regression on the 800 loans with nothing

146
00:06:12,382 --> 00:06:14,183
but the gradient we just derived.

147
00:06:14,410 --> 00:06:19,183
The loss per loan falls from 0.693 to 0.483.

148
00:06:19,375 --> 00:06:22,426
Now the readout a credit officer actually uses.

149
00:06:22,653 --> 00:06:25,504
Holding everything else fixed, a better current-account

150
00:06:25,366 --> 00:06:29,041
balance multiplies the odds of a bad loan by 0.47,

151
00:06:29,186 --> 00:06:30,528
so it roughly halves them.

152
00:06:30,721 --> 00:06:34,740
A longer duration multiplies them by 1.35, a larger

153
00:06:34,601 --> 00:06:38,784
amount by 1.27, and more savings by 0.71.

154
00:06:38,986 --> 00:06:41,647
Every one of these is a sentence you can defend

155
00:06:41,509 --> 00:06:42,980
in front of a regulator.

156
00:06:43,167 --> 00:06:44,178
Did it work?

157
00:06:44,372 --> 00:06:48,012
On the 200 unseen loans the model is right 74 percent

158
00:06:47,874 --> 00:06:51,810
of the time, against 71 percent for simply approving

159
00:06:51,671 --> 00:06:54,805
everyone, and its area under the curve is 0.74,

160
00:06:54,667 --> 00:06:57,515
which is where published results on this dataset

161
00:06:57,377 --> 00:06:58,175
sit.

162
00:06:58,037 --> 00:07:02,031
The calibration error is 0.074, so when it says

163
00:07:02,117 --> 00:07:04,732
thirty percent, about thirty percent go bad.

164
00:07:04,594 --> 00:07:08,364
And here is one real applicant from the test set:

165
00:07:08,226 --> 00:07:13,353
35 years old, a 36-month loan of 6,948 marks, with

166
00:07:13,214 --> 00:07:15,593
a current account under 200 marks.

167
00:07:15,455 --> 00:07:19,508
The model puts the risk at 56 percent, and the

168
00:07:19,370 --> 00:07:20,027
bank's threshold decides what happens next.

169
00:07:21,476 --> 00:07:24,492
Now the part most explanations skip: when these

170
00:07:24,353 --> 00:07:25,329
models fail.

171
00:07:25,542 --> 00:07:27,531
First, extrapolation.

172
00:07:27,735 --> 00:07:31,776
The hottest hour in the bike data was 41 degrees.

173
00:07:31,638 --> 00:07:34,536
Ask the model about sixty degrees and it happily

174
00:07:34,398 --> 00:07:38,192
predicts 577 rentals, more than at thirty, because

175
00:07:38,053 --> 00:07:40,160
a straight line never stops.

176
00:07:40,023 --> 00:07:43,061
A regression is only trustworthy inside the range

177
00:07:42,924 --> 00:07:43,863
it was trained on.

178
00:07:44,042 --> 00:07:45,642
Second, leakage.

179
00:07:45,870 --> 00:07:48,861
The bike table also records casual and registered

180
00:07:48,724 --> 00:07:51,751
rentals separately, and the two add up to the total.

181
00:07:51,955 --> 00:07:54,369
Put them in as features and the error drops to

182
00:07:54,231 --> 00:07:56,999
exactly zero, which looks like a triumph and is

183
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:59,109
actually a model that needs the answer in order

184
00:07:58,971 --> 00:08:00,194
to make its prediction.

185
00:08:00,422 --> 00:08:03,213
Whenever a result looks too good, ask whether a

186
00:08:03,075 --> 00:08:05,501
feature was known before the decision was made.

187
00:08:05,667 --> 00:08:08,163
Third, imbalance and the threshold.

188
00:08:08,379 --> 00:08:11,241
Seventy percent of the loans are good, so approving

189
00:08:11,103 --> 00:08:14,897
everyone is already 71 percent accurate, and accuracy

190
00:08:14,759 --> 00:08:16,242
alone would hide that.

191
00:08:16,103 --> 00:08:19,224
At a threshold of one half the model catches 48

192
00:08:19,087 --> 00:08:21,937
percent of the bad loans; lower it to zero point

193
00:08:21,799 --> 00:08:25,769
three and it catches 65 percent, at the cost of

194
00:08:25,632 --> 00:08:28,151
flagging 87 applicants instead of 49.

195
00:08:28,014 --> 00:08:30,876
The threshold is a business decision about which

196
00:08:31,009 --> 00:08:33,505
mistake costs more, not a mathematical constant.

197
00:08:33,542 --> 00:08:35,166
Fourth, the wrong shape.

198
00:08:35,028 --> 00:08:38,008
Look at the bike model's errors by hour of day.

199
00:08:37,870 --> 00:08:41,440
At eight in the morning it under-predicts by 237

200
00:08:41,502 --> 00:08:44,789
rentals, at five in the afternoon by 160, and at

201
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:47,843
three at night it over-predicts by 34: it cannot

202
00:08:47,705 --> 00:08:49,258
bend around rush hour.

203
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:52,076
When the residuals show a pattern like this, the

204
00:08:51,938 --> 00:08:54,753
relationship is not linear, and that, not fashion,

205
00:08:54,615 --> 00:08:57,347
is the signal to move to trees or a neural network.

206
00:08:58,809 --> 00:09:00,645
So here is the checklist.

207
00:09:00,836 --> 00:09:03,628
Decide what kind of answer you need: a number means

208
00:09:03,489 --> 00:09:06,245
linear regression, a yes or no means logistic,

209
00:09:06,355 --> 00:09:08,380
several classes means softmax.

210
00:09:08,594 --> 00:09:11,562
Build a table with one row per example, features

211
00:09:11,424 --> 00:09:14,310
known at decision time, and a held-out test set.

212
00:09:14,525 --> 00:09:17,223
Fit the regression first, read the coefficients

213
00:09:17,084 --> 00:09:19,794
as plain sentences, and judge it on the test set

214
00:09:19,655 --> 00:09:22,541
with the right metric: error for a number, area

215
00:09:22,403 --> 00:09:24,912
under the curve and calibration for a probability.

216
00:09:25,125 --> 00:09:28,211
Then check the four ways it fails: never extrapolate

217
00:09:28,073 --> 00:09:30,617
outside the data, hunt for leaked features when

218
00:09:30,479 --> 00:09:33,070
a result looks too good, choose the threshold by

219
00:09:32,932 --> 00:09:35,428
the cost of each mistake, and look at the residuals

220
00:09:35,290 --> 00:09:37,634
for a pattern before you reach for a deeper model.

221
00:09:37,837 --> 00:09:40,264
Do that, and you have done what the bank and the

222
00:09:40,125 --> 00:09:41,726
bike company actually do.

223
00:09:41,941 --> 00:09:45,063
Next: the neuron and activation functions.
